<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hebei</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hebei is a province of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> surrounding present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> it was an important region for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-smelting">iron production</a> on a large scale. It also lay on the frontier with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and later fell to the Jin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hebei.jpg" alt="Hebei" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Whereisthetruthicantfindyou · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fengning_Jing_Bei_meadow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hebei means north of the river, from its position north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>. In the Yu Gong the region is recorded as Jizhou, which gave it the abbreviation Ji, and its nickname Yanzhao comes from the states of Yan and Zhao that held it in antiquity. The west of the province rises into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taihang-mountains">Taihang Mountains</a> and the Yan Mountains run through the north, beyond which lie the grasslands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>. The Bohai Sea lies to the east, and the province entirely surrounds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a> on land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Warring States period Jin was partitioned and much of its territory in Hebei went to Zhao. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> the area was ruled as You Prefecture in the north and Ji Province in the south. At the end of the Han, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a> overcame Gongsun Zan there but was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-guandu">Battle of Guandu</a> in 200. The northern frontier position made Hebei change hands often in the age of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> had its capital at Ye, near modern Linzhang. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> the area was officially called Hebei for the first time, and a Great Yan State existed there from 756 to 763 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a>.</p><p>Emperor Gaozu of the Later Jin ceded much of northern Hebei to the Khitan Liao dynasty. These <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a> of Yanyun, which lay within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a>, became a weakness in Chinese defence for the next century. After the Jingkang Incident of 1127 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> gave up all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china">North China</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchen-jin-dynasty">Jurchen Jin dynasty</a>. Between 1048 and 1128 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> ran directly through the province. Under the Yuan the area was administered directly from the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>. In the Ming it became Beizhili, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1421. The Qing called it Zhili, meaning directly ruled, and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> created the province of Hebei in 1928, when the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hebei is a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china">North China</a> with Shijiazhuang as its capital and largest city; its population was 74.6 million at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. After the founding of the People's Republic, the regions around Chengde and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhangjiakou">Zhangjiakou</a> were merged into it. The Tangshan earthquake of 28 July 1976 killed about 242,000 people by the official count. Five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> lie there, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a>, the Chengde Mountain Resort and the Qing imperial tombs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anji-bridge">Zhaozhou Bridge</a>, built during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a>, is the oldest standing bridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hebei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…former subjects who built a khaganate on the steppe by 402; their raids were constant, and in 423 a wall over 2,000 li (1,080 kilometres) was built from Chicheng in <b>Hebei</b> to Wuyuan in Inner Mongolia, the first Great Wall since the Han, and a second in 446.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Coal and iron production in the north, especially in <b>Hebei</b> and Shanxi, reached scales that Europe would not surpass until the eighteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 2nd century the southern chanyu sent his people against the Yellow Turban Rebellion and then against the Wuhuan in <b>Hebei</b> in 188.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its remnants scattered to western Sichuan, northern Tibet and to Henan and <b>Hebei</b>, where they persisted into the middle of the Ming.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Builders raised town walls of rammed earth and improved the making of wells: the Hougang II variant of northern Henan and southern <b>Hebei</b> has the first wells of the Yellow River area, dug by a method that early Bronze Age states there went on using.</blockquote><a href="/culture/longshan-culture">Longshan culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>A 2014 repair with concrete near the border of Liaoning and <b>Hebei</b> was much criticised, and in 2023 workers in Shanxi widened a gap for an excavator, causing what police called “irreversible damage”.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall of China · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
